WoW emulation has always been interesting to me, and a server emulator turns out
to be a good test of what a cluster is actually for. Almost all of one is
ordinary stateless plumbing that belongs in
[the k3s platform](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/). One process does not, and that one
is the game.

These projects exist to study game server architecture and network protocol
design, and that is the only use I am discussing. If you run anything, the
legality of it is your own responsibility.

::: note
**Part 2 of 2 of [Where to start with
self-hosting](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/).** Previous: [K3s
starter kit on Hetzner cloud](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/)
:::

## The shape

Three pieces, and the same three in TrinityCore, AzerothCore, CMaNGOS, MaNGOS
and AscEmu: an authentication daemon that answers the login handshake, a world
daemon that is the game, and a relational database split into auth, characters
and world content.

## What goes in the cluster

- The authentication daemon. Its durable state is in the database, so the
  process itself is close to stateless.
- The website, the registration API, the account panel: ordinary CRUD over that
  same database.
- Item shops, armory pages, ladders, statistics. Read-mostly and cacheable.
- Discord bots, status pages, uptime checks.
- Backups, schema migrations and content-database imports, which is what Jobs
  and CronJobs are for.
- Observability for all of the above, and for the world machine too, which can
  push into the cluster without living in it.

That is an ordinary small platform, and it is the shape the
[k3s kit](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/) generates: GitOps delivery, a namespace per
tenant, certificates, secrets, observability.

## What does not

The world daemon, for four reasons, and any one of them would be enough.

- **Its tick is bounded by a single thread.** Parallelism exists between maps
  and instances but never inside one, and `MapManager` puts a barrier on every
  tick, so the tick costs whatever the slowest individual map costs. TrinityCore
  ships `MapUpdate.Threads = 1` and CMaNGOS ships 3. What it wants is clock
  speed, and Kubernetes hands out shared, movable CPU unless you go to the
  trouble of the `static` policy on a dedicated node, which is a private machine
  with extra steps.
- **The state is in RAM and it grows.** AzerothCore's memory page says maps are
  cached as players explore them and never freed until a restart, ending past
  11 GB, and recommends starting at 16 GB. Restarting a pod restarts the server
  for everyone logged in.
- **Readiness means nothing here.** A world server that has finished loading is
  not interchangeable with the one it replaced, so a rolling update has nothing
  to roll over to.
- **The connection is a long-lived raw TCP session**, not a request, which is
  the opposite of what ingress controllers, retries and connection draining
  assume.

Cheap shared VPS instances are the obvious way to get this wrong. I ran servers
on them years ago and the symptom matched the code above: fine most of the time,
then not, with cores sitting there that nothing could spend.

## The join decides your topology

The auth daemon and the world daemon are connected by a database table rather
than an API. TrinityCore's auth database has a `realmlist` row per realm with
`address` and `port`
([realmlist](https://github.com/TrinityCore/tc-wiki/blob/main/database/335/auth/realmlist.md)),
documented as the world server's public address. The client logs in, gets that
address, and opens a raw TCP connection straight to the world daemon.

So there is no front door doing the routing, and whatever you put in front of
the world machine has to be a transparent TCP path. The address can be a
hostname rather than an IP, but the client resolves it and opens a raw socket,
so there is no HTTP layer for an ingress to route on. Proxy it at layer 4 or not
at all.

## Building the world machine

Nothing about that part is worth re-deriving here. CMaNGOS's [installation
instructions](https://github.com/cmangos/issues/wiki/Installation-Instructions)
cover Debian and Ubuntu end to end: the package list, the CMake build, the three
databases and the script that populates them, extracting maps and DBC files from
a game client, and the realm configuration that ties it together.

Budget a fast core, plenty of RAM, a local disk and a short path to your
players, in that order. The database is also where the time actually goes: the
servers I ran broke on data far more often than on code, with a world database
half-migrated by whoever was developing against it, DBC files mangled on
extraction, or a missing MPQ archive making the maps come out wrong.

## Where the line falls

Split the system by whether a process can be killed and replaced without anyone
noticing. Auth, web, shop, armory, statistics, bots, backups and jobs all pass
that test, and a cluster gives them reconciled configuration, generated
isolation and one place to look when something breaks.

The world daemon fails it, and it is the only thing that does. None of which is
an argument against Kubernetes. It is an argument against assuming one system
has one shape, which is the assumption that puts the world server in a pod in
the first place.
